.TH LIBMPQ__ARCHIVE_CREATE_IO 3 2026-11-18 "The MoPaQ archive library"
.SH NAME
libmpq__archive_create_io \- create an MPQ in caller-owned storage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <libmpq/mpq.h>
.sp
.BI "int32_t libmpq__archive_create_io("
.BI "        mpq_archive_s **" "archive,"
.BI "        void *" "context,"
.BI "        libmpq_read_at_fn " "read_at,"
.BI "        libmpq_write_at_fn " "write_at,"
.BI "        libmpq_flush_fn " "flush,"
.BI "        const mpq_archive_create_options_s *" "options"
.BI ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Create a v1, v2, v3, or v4 MPQ in an initially empty seekable output at
logical origin zero. The caller supplies write-at and any required read-at
capability. NULL options select the defaults of
.BR libmpq__archive_create (3).
V4 creation requires read-at, including if RAW chunks will later be
disabled. Unsigned v1 through v3 may omit it. Signing requires read-at.
Weak signing remains unavailable for v4 with RAW chunks enabled.
.PP
Read and write callbacks receive a borrowed byte buffer, nonnegative signed
64-bit offset and native \fIsize_t\fP length. Return zero only after transferring
the entire range. Complete short transfers in the adapter or return a
negative libmpq error. Successful writes preserve unrelated bytes and
extend storage to the maximum written end. Seeking and zero-byte transfers
do not extend storage. The sink must supply zero-filled gaps and make prior
writes visible to subsequent reads. Sparse gaps may remain unallocated.
.PP
The library borrows the context and callbacks through consuming archive
close, including failed close. NULL context is permitted for stateless
callbacks. The library never closes, frees, truncates, deletes or publishes
the sink. The calling thread runs callbacks synchronously. Callbacks must
not re-enter the same writer. Serialize all writer calls and shared
storage access externally.
.PP
Use the existing member writer for incremental input. Oversized SINGLE members
still require a complete input unit in memory and may require codec workspace
and another packed buffer. Library buffering is separate from any memory used
by the sink.
.PP
If supplied, flush runs once after successful metadata and signature
finalization. It is skipped on earlier failure. Flush is not a read-back
barrier. The caller controls persistence, disposal and publication. Errors
can leave partial bytes. There is no rollback, atomic publication or
durability guarantee.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Zero on success. Failed creation clears the output pointer when provided.
Missing output, write-at or required read-at returns
.BR LIBMPQ_ERROR_EXIST .
Invalid creation options return
.BR LIBMPQ_ERROR_FORMAT .
Unrepresentable layouts return
.BR LIBMPQ_ERROR_SIZE .
Allocation failures return
.BR LIBMPQ_ERROR_MALLOC .
.PP
Negative callback errors propagate. Positive callback statuses map to
.BR LIBMPQ_ERROR_READ ,
.BR LIBMPQ_ERROR_WRITE ,
or
.B LIBMPQ_ERROR_CLOSE
according to the operation. A terminal callback or admitted member failure
latches the first error, preventing further output callbacks. Finishing an
incomplete member fails and latches SIZE. Closing with an active member and
no prior error returns SIZE. Close consumes the archive even on failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libmpq (3),
.BR libmpq__archive_create (3),
.BR libmpq__archive_open_io (3),
.BR libmpq__archive_close (3),
.BR libmpq__archive_sign (3),
.BR libmpq__writer_begin (3)
